{"sections":[],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"reingoldTilfordLayout","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:12GraphDrawing0B0V","text":"Drawing","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/Drawing","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"text":"Discussion","level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The root is at (0, 0) and a vertex at depth d has y = d. Children are placed left to right"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"in "},{"code":"children(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" order, vertices at one depth at least 1 apart along each subtree’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"contour, and every parent centred over its first and last child. Where two subtrees are"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"pushed apart, the smaller subtrees between them are spaced evenly. A mirrored tree draws as"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the reflection of the drawing, and isomorphic subtrees are drawn identically up to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"translation. Separations and centring give multiples of a power of ½, which are exact in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"Double","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". The even spacing divides a shift by the number of gaps, so values such as ±5\/6"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"occur and are rounded, and a mirrored tree’s drawing then matches the reflection only to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"rounding."}]},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Reingold and Tilford, “Tidier drawings of trees”, IEEE Transactions on Software","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Engineering SE-7(2), 1981.","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Walker, “A node-positioning algorithm for general trees”, Software: Practice and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Experience 20(7), 1990.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Buchheim, Jünger and Leipert, “Improving Walker’s algorithm to run in linear time”,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Graph Drawing (GD 2002), LNCS 2528, 2002.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"O(n) time and memory, with no recursion.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/Trees\/RootedTree\/reingoldTilfordLayout()"},"abstract":[{"text":"The tidy drawing of the tree: Reingold and Tilford’s layered tree drawing as Walker","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"refined it, in Buchheim, Jünger and Leipert’s linear-time form.","type":"text"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing","doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/Trees","doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/Trees\/RootedTree"]]},"metadata":{"externalID":"s:5Trees10RootedTreeV12GraphDrawingE21reingoldTilfordLayoutAD0E0VyxGyF","modules":[{"name":"GraphDrawing","relatedModules":["Trees"]}],"conformance":{"availabilityPrefix":[{"text":"Available when","type":"text"}],"conformancePrefix":[{"text":"Conforms when","type":"text"}],"constraints":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Vertex"},{"text":" conforms to ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hashable"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},"roleHeading":"Instance Method","role":"symbol","extendedModule":"Trees","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"re